 that  are looking around saying well there are  no hot dots so what do i do  so  they are  probably selling some things like all  the the nasty stuff about the hedge  funds and the tiger cubs and all that  stuff there are people they're going to  try to redeem from those things that's  probably a mistake  um and one of the things about investing  that that i tried to live and i was  actually decent at it but not not  perfect  but  what you should do  you should never buy something that has  had a hot three-year period  you should sell that because it's going  to mean revert what you should do is  find somebody  who has a great 10 year track record  who's just had a terrible  one two three year run like and the  worst the better  and give that person money so there are  some that are out there doing that and  those are the the real geniuses  i think the last thing is  i i think some of this being talked  about at the institutional level a lot  right now  is the same thing as being talked about  at the individual level which is well  wait a second this is different what  about all these assets that were  supposed to be uncorrelated why are they  all going down at the same time i  thought bonds were supposed to not go  down if stocks went down they're  supposed to be a safe haven i thought  gold was supposed to be a safe haven i  thought you know crypto is supposed to  be a safe haven i thought international  stocks weren't supposed to be correlated  with u.s stocks well here's the problem  the everything bubble which was the  result of the excessive central bank  liquidity around the world caused all  assets to move  to stupidville right you mentioned the  market cap to gdp right worse than 2000  price to sales ratio worse than 2000 so  equities got to stupidville bonds listen  to greg foss on twitter right rail about  this got to stupidville  and everything commodities and all of it  went up and so what happened is when  they cut off the liquidity  the first thing s